<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Post-Angkorian Cambodia</h1><p class="st-meta">1431 CE – 1863 CE · capital Longvek</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Khmer kingdom between the abandonment of Angkor and French protection, ruled from Longvek and Oudong and squeezed between Siam and Vietnam.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya's</a> sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> around 1431, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> court under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ponhea-yat">Ponhea Yat</a> moved south toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonle-sap">Tonle Sap</a> confluence, eventually settling at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ang-chan-i">Ang Chan I</a> in the 16th century the capital moved to Longvek, and later to Oudong.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom drew wealth from river trade at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaktomuk">Quatre Bras</a>, with Chinese, Malay, Japanese, Portuguese and Spanish merchants present. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> replaced the older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> cults, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reamker">Reamker</a> version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> flourished. Royal factions sought Siamese or Vietnamese backing, and Spanish adventurers intervened in the 1590s. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> captured Longvek in 1594.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 17th century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a> gained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> delta, and in the 1830s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a> occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Cambodia</a> until a Siamese-Vietnamese settlement of the mid-1840s installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ang-duong">Ang Duong</a>. His son Norodom accepted the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-protectorate-of-cambodia">French protectorate of Cambodia</a> in 1863.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/post-angkorian-cambodia.jpg" alt="Post-Angkorian Cambodia" width="320" height="504"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Eynaud · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southeast_Asian_history_-_Around_1540.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1431 CE – 1863 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Longvek</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ponhea Yat, Ang Chan I, Ang Duong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Khmer</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Middle Period of Cambodia, Kingdom of Cambodia (1431-1863), Longvek, Oudong</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1647&amp;focus=post-angkorian-cambodia" aria-label="Post-Angkorian Cambodia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7560 1735 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Post-Angkorian Cambodia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7880 1991L7846 1933M7880 1991L7881 2044M7880 1991L7886 2002M7880 1991L7752 1900M7880 1991L7754 1922M7880 1991L7930 1822"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7846 1933h0M7881 2044h0M7886 2002h0M7752 1900h0M7754 1922h0M7930 1822h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7880 1991h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7880 1991h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Longvek. <a href="/atlas?year=1647&amp;focus=post-angkorian-cambodia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
